hi there steve kaufman here um  i recently did a video where i talked  about how our emotions get in the way  of our language learning  that um you know even though we would  like to pronounce say something a  certain way i use the example of myself  learning portuguese and and  speaking with brazilian people and  hearing them  say bongia and because of my spanish i  just don't want to say bungie even  though that's how how it's said  uh you know that my wife doesn't want to  pronounce the th  in spanish  uh there's a number of things that  whether it be because of our culture or  because of whatever prejudices we may  have  or other emotional  sort of forms of resistance  these things get in the way of us simply  accepting the new language and just  going with it and flowing with it i  mentioned the example of my father who  always used to say nova scotia instead  of nova scotia because to him it just  seemed to make more sense that it be  pronounced like the original latin or  something that he wasn't going to say  nova scotia even though he heard it all  the time and this kind of stuff happens  regularly and um  there was a person who commented on that  video and said you know a lot of the  resistance the emotional resistance that  we have to different aspects of the  language that we're trying to learn  a lot of this is subconscious so how do  we  persuade our brains  not to resist emotionally  uh surely that's impossible sort of  thing was his question  and  so  i'm going to tackle that one  first of all i think we have to decide  whether or not we want to change  this emotional resistance  let's take an example i have a certain  way of learning a language it's based on  listening and reading  and  sort of discovering the language  naturally acquiring words getting a feel  for the language  if i'm confronted with a system that  asks me to  i was on a system the other day just to  see what the competition is doing  where they  uh  show you pictures and then give you  these little snippets of phrases then  they quickly turn around and give it to  you in english and ask you to  say it in the target language then they  have you dragging and dropping words  while you're doing something with word  order in other words there is this whole  pedagogical school of thought that says  that we have to get the student to do  something with the language respond  say something  move words around analyze  and i have a tremendous resistance to  this the minute i see that i'm out of  there i don't want it i don't want to  have to say anything until i've heard it  a lot until i've had a chance to absorb  it so this might in fact be an  irrational emotional reaction on my part  i don't know but i have no desire to  change it  so  there's no way there that i'm going to  change it because i'm quite comfortable  i enjoy doing it my way i'm not about to  change  however  there  let's take the case of the  brazilian pronunciation there i think my  reaction is much more irrational  and so how do we change  emotional resistance that we have  uh  to something  i think in many cases if i if i look at  immigrants here who never progress  beyond a certain level and say english  it's because they're not very motivated  or they want to kind of hang back in  their culture  so  you know how do they how do we get them  to change that well if they don't want  to change it you can't get them to  change it  but  uh if in fact at some level we want to  change  then i think it can be done and that's  where i want to bring in what i consider  a very interesting book on many levels  which i have mentioned before it's  called the mind and the brain  the mind and the brain by let me just  hold it up a little bit better here the  mind and the brain by basically jeffrey  schwartz and what this book does it  talks about people who have  obsessive-compulsive behavior  and obsessive-compulsive behavior  it describes a situation where people  feel they have to wash their hands every  five minutes or  i don't know they're afraid of snakes it  could be anything which becomes  obsessive and very often this is treated  with drugs  and jeffrey schwartz developed  an approach a treatment  which is essentially based on getting  the patient to recognize  that the neural network that is driving  him or her to wash their hands every  three minutes or do ever something else  that's obsessive  that that sort of exists objectively in  the brain  and that with enough determination  and with a series of exercises and  practice  that the  mind  of of the patient  the determination the will the willing  to willingness to recognize that this  this circuitry that causes this person  to behave in a certain way  exists objectively and is not  necessarily part of that person's  personality  or or it doesn't have to be part of his  or her behavior  so that once the person recognizes that  their mind can be apart from  this behavior  then they can start looking at ways that  the mind can influence the brain and  actually change those neural networks  change that behavior or as the person  commenting to my previous video  said that we have these emotional  resistances  and that they are subconscious but even  subconscious things in the brain if we  are determined to  we can actually influence them through  our determination  so  if we are resisting something  hanging back in our native culture  unwilling to basically let ourselves go  let's say we have the irrational fear of  making a mistake or creating a bad  impression  i mean to make  the the fear that we're going to make a  mistake is not irrational but the fear  that we will be somehow thought less  well of or that it somehow causes people  not to like us or any of these other  fears associated with making a mistake  are largely irrational and so we can  perhaps using some of the  the methodology that's described by  schwartz here influence our brain so  that we are less  concerned less uptight less afraid or  that we don't resist certain forms of  pronunciation or  certain structures  uh of the new language and then we just  you know wholeheartedly  take them up and make them make them  part of ourselves  so given that you want to make that  change at some level and that the brain  is resisting the mind in fact according  to jeffrey schwartz can overcome the  brain  and i also have mentioned before in a  video that  i had a little bit of experience of  something somewhat related to this  when i was a little boy my father could  wiggle his ears  and i wanted to be able to wiggle my  ears  and i would literally sit  for hours  just  telling myself you know wiggle the ears  and eventually i was able to cause  a neural network or some connection to  occur  in my brain  that i don't know if you can see this  but i can wiggle my ears especially i  think this one no it's this one here  that wiggles more  you probably can't see it but i can  assure you i can wiggle my ears and this  is something that i achieved through my  determination  the mind  influencing the brain  so  uh i think that that uh the the answer  to this question uh you know how do we  overcome these irrational fears that we  have or these emotional  forms of emotional resistance that we  have well if you read the book by  jeffrey schwartz  uh you will see that in fact our  determination which is sending some kind  of uh you know electric electrochemical  signals  through the neurons and i mean i don't  understand all that stuff but it can  affect  the behavior of the brain the mind can  influence the brain and that i think has  lots of significance for language  learning so that's my answer to that  person's question thank you for  listening
 

